  "body": "This allows responsive image selection to account for both dimensions and object fitting (`contain` / `cover`), fixing source selection when an image is constrained by height or `object-fit`.\n\n- Re-introduces the `<height-descriptor>` (`<positive-integer>h`) alongside `<width-descriptor>` (`w`) in `srcset` image candidate strings so each candidate's aspect ratio can be determined before fetching.\n- Extends `<source-size-value>` to accept `[ contain | cover ] <length> <length>`, representing a fit mode, width, and height. Bare 2D `<source-size>` items without a `<media-condition>` are permitted before the final item in a `<source-size-list>` without a parse error so authors can provide a 1D fallback for legacy user agents (e.g., `sizes=\"cover 400px 400px, 400px\"`).\n- Instead of using the `concrete object size` width (which depends on the already-loaded image's natural aspect ratio or causes inconsistent selection with `object-fit: cover` / `contain`), `sizes=\"auto\"` now resolves to a `source size` struct using the `img` element's `content box` width and height and its computed `'object-fit'` value (`'contain'`/`'scale-down'` -> `\"contain\"`, `'cover'` -> `\"cover\"`, `'fill'`/`'none'` -> `\"fill\"`).\n- Computes each candidate's aspect ratio from its `w` and `h` descriptors (falling back to the `dimension attribute aspect ratio` from `width`/`height` attributes on the element, derives the candidate's `source concrete width` given the `source size`'s `width`, `height`, and `fit`, and normalizes the candidate's pixel density using that concrete width prior to `select an image source from a source set` (covering `<img>`, `<picture><source>`, `<link rel=\"preload\" as=\"image\">`, and `Link:` HTTP preload headers).\n- Tracks `width` and `height` attribute mutations on `<img>` and preceding `<source>` elements in `<picture>`, and for `sizes=\"auto\"`, reacts to changes in the `img`'s `content box` width or height or computed `'object-fit'` property.\n\nFixes #12248.\nFixes #2973.\n\n---\n\n<!-- Please complete the following checklist; see https://whatwg.org/working-mode#additions for more details: -->\n\n- [x] At least two implementers are interested (and none opposed):\n   * Chromium\n   * WebKit / Gecko (discussed in #12248)\n- [x] [Tests](https://github.com/web-platform-tests/wpt) are written and can be reviewed and commented upon at:\n   * https://github.com/web-platform-tests/wpt/pull/64025\n- [x] [Implementation bugs](https://github.com/whatwg/meta/blob/main/MAINTAINERS.md#handling-pull-requests) are filed:\n   * Chromium: https://issues.chromium.org/issues/572724710\n   * Gecko: TBD\n   * WebKit: TBD\n- [ ] [MDN issue](https://github.com/whatwg/meta/blob/main/MAINTAINERS.md#handling-pull-requests) is filed: …\n- [x] The top of this comment includes a [clear commit message](https://github.com/whatwg/html/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md#commit-messages) to use.\n\n<!-- You are welcome to delete this template if this is not a normative change that impacts implementers. -->\n\n<!-- The following line is used by the HTML Standard's PR preview Bot to add a link to the preview of your pull request. Please do not remove it. -->\n<!-- UNDER NO CIRCUMSTANCES DELETE THIS LINE -->\n(This comment was updated by the HTML Standard PR Preview Bot.)\n\n\n<!--\n    This comment and the below content is programmatically generated.\n    You may add a comma-separated list of anchors you'd like a\n    direct link to below (e.g. #idl-serializers, #idl-sequence):\n\n    Don't remove this comment or modify anything below this line.\n    If you don't want a preview generated for this pull request,\n    just replace the whole of this comment's content by \"no preview\"\n    and remove what's below.\n-->\n***\n<a href=\"https://whatpr.org/html/13053/embedded-content.html\" title=\"Last updated on Nov 6, 2026, 10:14 PM UTC (63bb098)\">/embedded-content.html</a>  ( <a href=\"https://whatpr.org/html/13053/5955a33...63bb098/embedded-content.ht